= Assa-peixe

_Status: a Brazilian native_ Asteraceae _of the_ Vernonia / Vernonanthura _genus —_ Vernonanthura polyanthes _(Spreng.) A.J.Vega & Dematt. (older synonym_ Vernonia polyanthes _Less.) — foundational to Brazilian folk medicine as the canonical_ respiratory aromatic-and-bitter, _principally applied to chronic bronchitis, asthma, persistent cough, and inflammatory pulmonary conditions. The species is exclusively Brazilian and post-Columbian to all four canonical Eurasian medical traditions; the substantive tradition is_ Brazilian folk-medical and indigenous _across the Cerrado, the Atlantic Forest, and the broader Brazilian heartland. The vernacular name_ assa-peixe _("fish-roaster") preserves the_ indigenous-Tupi-and-caboclo cooking-and-medicine tradition _of using the dried inflorescences as fuel-or-skewer for fish-cooking, with the leaves wrapping the fish in some regional preparations. Related Brazilian species — including_ Vernonanthura phosphorica _and_ Vernonia ferruginea _— share the assa-peixe vernacular in some regional usages, creating species-identification ambiguity._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Vernonanthura polyanthes_ (Spreng.) A.J.Vega & Dematt., family Asteraceae (Compositae). Older synonyms include _Vernonia polyanthes_ Less., _Cacalia polyanthes_ Spreng. The drug is principally the *aerial parts* — leaves, flowering tops, and tender stems; some traditional preparations use the *bark* and the *root*. The genus _Vernonanthura_ was *segregated from Vernonia* in the 1992 phylogenetic revision (Robinson) for the South-American clade; the older botanical literature on assa-peixe is uniformly under _Vernonia_. The closely-related Brazilian _Vernonia_ / _Vernonanthura_ species variously called assa-peixe:
- *_Vernonanthura phosphorica_ (Vell.) H.Rob.* — another widely-used Brazilian species.
- *_Vernonia ferruginea_ Less.* — Cerrado region.
- *_Vernonia condensata_ Baker* — *boldo-da-bahia* (a different vernacular, but in the same genus).
- *_Vernonia amygdalina_ Delile* — *African bitter leaf*; not Brazilian, but the genus's most-studied member globally.

The genus *_Vernonia_* commemorates *William Vernon* (d. 1711), English botanist who collected in Maryland. *_Vernonanthura_* combines _Vernonia_ + Greek ἀνθήρ (_anthḗr_, "anther / male flower").#footnote[Species identity and synonymy: _Flora do Brasil_ (Jardim Botânico do Rio de Janeiro); Robinson, _Generic and Subtribal Classification of American Vernonieae_ (1999, _Smithsonian Contributions to Botany_); modern Brazilian regional ethnobotanical literature including the surveys of Lorenzi and Matos. The genus_ Vernonanthura _segregation from_ Vernonia _is 1992 (Robinson); older Brazilian-folk literature uses_ Vernonia polyanthes _throughout.]

*English:* No widely-established English common name; *Brazilian-Cerrado-vernonia*, *Brazilian-bitter-leaf* (by analogy with the African _V. amygdalina_) appear in specialty botanical literature; *assa-peixe* is also used in English specialty herbal-medicine literature.

*Portuguese (Brazilian):* *assa-peixe* — the canonical name; literally *"fish-roaster"* from Portuguese _assar_ ("to roast") + _peixe_ ("fish"). The vernacular preserves an *indigenous-Tupi-and-caboclo cooking tradition* in which the dried inflorescence-stalks were used as *fish-roasting skewers* (the strong woody stalks burn slowly with aromatic smoke that flavors the fish), and in which the *broad leaves were used to wrap fish* before underground-cooking or pit-roasting in some regional traditions. The substance is therefore *culinary-and-medical* at the boundary, with the cooking-tradition naming the medicinal-tradition substance. Regional variants and related vernaculars: *assa-peixe-do-cerrado*, *assa-peixe-branco* (white-flowering form, the most common assa-peixe), *assa-peixe-roxo* (purple-flowering, _V. phosphorica_), *cambará-açu* (a Tupi-derived alternative used in some Minas Gerais and São Paulo regions, with substantial vernacular overlap with other Asteraceae). Note: the vernacular _cambará_ alone is most commonly applied to _Lantana camara_ (Verbenaceae, the fully unrelated _Cambará vermelho_), creating substantial species-identification ambiguity in regional Brazilian-Portuguese ethnobotanical contexts. Specify the binomial.

*Spanish:* No widespread Spanish-American vernacular; *vernonia brasileña* in technical Argentine-Uruguayan literature.

*Tupi-Guarani:* *cambará* or *cambará-açu* — the broader Tupi name applied to several Asteraceae and other Brazilian native shrubs, with substantial polysemy. The Tupi-language register precedes the Portuguese *assa-peixe* naming and may itself be the original indigenous name from which the cooking-association developed.

*Sanskrit / Hindi:* No canonical entry — the species is exclusively American. The genus _Vernonia_ has a different species (_V. anthelmintica_, _kālijīrī_ / _bakuchi-vernonia_) in classical Ayurvedic use, but this is a distinct South-Asian species, not assa-peixe.

*Chinese:* No canonical entry. The genus _Vernonia_ has Asian species but no canonical _Vernonanthura polyanthes_ entry.

*Arabic / Persian:* No canonical Tibb entry — exclusively American species.

== Classical Brazilian / Indigenous / Caboclo tradition

_This is the substantive tradition for assa-peixe._ The substance is foundational to Brazilian rural-and-suburban folk medicine — especially in *Minas Gerais, São Paulo, Goiás, Mato Grosso, Bahia*, and the broader Cerrado-and-Atlantic-Forest Brazilian heartland — and is one of the most-cited substances in modern Brazilian ethnobotanical surveys for respiratory complaints. The Brazilian Pharmacopoeia (Farmacopeia Brasileira) and the Anvisa-approved *RENISUS* (Relação Nacional de Plantas Medicinais de Interesse ao SUS, the National Relation of Medicinal Plants of Interest to the Brazilian Public Health System) include assa-peixe.

*Indigenous Tupi-and-Guarani register:* the dried inflorescence-stalks used as *fish-roasting skewers* (giving the Portuguese name); the leaves as *fish-wrapping for cooking*; the leaves and flowers as a *medicinal infusion* for respiratory complaints (chronic cough, bronchitis, asthma) and as a *topical* for wounds and skin disorders.

*Caboclo (rural-creole) register:* the same uses, with the addition of:
- *Chá de assa-peixe* — the canonical *Brazilian respiratory infusion* — for chronic bronchitis, asthma, persistent cough (especially the productive-phlegmatic cough), and general respiratory weakness. Sometimes paired with *guaco* (_Mikania glomerata_, the canonical Brazilian antitussive — see _guaco.typ_) and *eucalipto* (eucalyptus) for combined respiratory action.
- *Garrafada de assa-peixe* — Brazilian folk-medical decoction in a glass bottle; the substance often combined with other Brazilian native herbs in compound preparations.
- *Mel de assa-peixe* — the *honey produced by bees foraging on assa-peixe* — *a foundational Brazilian artisanal honey type*, with a distinctive dark color, mild bitter undertone, and strong respiratory-folk-medical valuation. The assa-peixe-blooming-season honey is collector-grade in Minas Gerais and São Paulo apiculture, with substantial commercial standing.
- *Tópico para feridas* — macerated leaves applied to wounds (the broader Asteraceae-topical-medicine register).
- *Banho de assa-peixe* — bath / wash preparations for skin and respiratory support; the Afro-Brazilian / Candomblé / Umbanda *banho* ritual register may include assa-peixe in some contexts (specific to particular religious traditions).

*Brazilian Pharmacopoeia and RENISUS:* assa-peixe entered the Brazilian Pharmacopoeia and modern phytotherapy register in the late 20th c.; the Anvisa-approved phytotherapeutic products include assa-peixe-based respiratory preparations alongside guaco.#footnote[Brazilian ethnobotanical and pharmacopoeial literature: Lorenzi & Matos, _Plantas Medicinais no Brasil_; Brazilian Pharmacopoeia (Farmacopeia Brasileira); Anvisa RENISUS — Relação Nacional de Plantas Medicinais de Interesse ao SUS; standard regional Brazilian folk-medicine literature. The assa-peixe-honey commercial tradition: standard apiculture literature for Minas Gerais and São Paulo.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Not classical for_ Vernonanthura polyanthes _specifically. Reasoned cross-system placement only._

By reasoned placement on the bitter-aromatic-Asteraceae axis: cool (涼) to neutral (平); bitter (苦), pungent (辛), aromatic (芳香); Lung (肺), Liver (肝), Spleen (脾). Reasoned actions: 清熱化痰 (clears heat and transforms phlegm — the canonical respiratory register), 止咳平喘 (stops cough, calms wheezing), 解毒 (resolves toxicity), 散結 (dissolves nodules). The reasoned register parallels the canonical Chinese 紫菀 / 款冬花 (zǐ wǎn / kuǎn dōng huā) antitussive-expectorant Asteraceae class. Reasoned, not citation.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Not classical for the Brazilian species. The related_ Vernonia anthelmintica _("kālijīrī," a South-Asian species) is canonical in Ayurveda — but is a distinct species in a different vernonia clade. Reasoned cross-system placement only._

*Reasoned rasa:* Tikta (bitter — strongly), Kaṭu (pungent), Kaṣāya (astringent — moderate).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (light), Rūkṣa (dry), Tīkṣṇa (sharp), Sūkṣma (subtle).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (warming — modest).

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (pungent).

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Kapha (strongly — the bitter-pungent-warming axis is the canonical Kapha-clearing direction, the central indication being chronic Kapha-pattern bronchitis and asthma); pacifies Vāta in moderate dose; aggravates Pitta in excess.

*Reasoned karma:* _Kāsa-hara, śvāsa-hara_ (the canonical respiratory action, vindicated by Brazilian folk practice); _kapha-hara_; _krimi-ghna_ (anthelminthic — modest, by extension on the bitter-Vernonia-genus register); _dīpana, pācana_ (digestive bitter); _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ (topical wound-healing); _viṣaghna_ (antitoxin, modest).

The substance does not appear in Caraka, Suśruta, or the major nighaṇṭus; the placement is reasoned cross-system.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(F) Conditional / Brazilian folk:* Pitta excess; pregnancy (the broader Asteraceae-bitter caution); known Asteraceae allergy; chronic prolonged use (the pyrrolizidine concern below).
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Pyrrolizidine alkaloids (PAs)*: *_Vernonanthura polyanthes_ and several related Brazilian Vernonia / Vernonanthura species contain pyrrolizidine alkaloids*, with documented *hepatotoxic and venous-occlusive-disease potential* at chronic high-dose exposure. Published Brazilian phytochemistry has identified hepatotoxic 1,2-unsaturated PAs in assa-peixe; *the substance should not be used in chronic / prolonged daily-tea regimens*, and high-dose / concentrated extract use is contraindicated. Acute / short-term use of standard infusion preparations is considered acceptable in modern Brazilian phytotherapy, but the chronic-use caveat is universal in current Anvisa and Brazilian Pharmacopoeia guidance.
  - *Asteraceae cross-allergy*: documented; common cross-reactivity with ragweed, chamomile, daisy.
  - *Pregnancy*: *contraindicated* — the PA-hepatotoxic concern combined with the broader Asteraceae-bitter emmenagogue pattern.
  - *Hepatic disease*: *contraindicated* — the PA hepatotoxicity concern is the principal modern safety overlay.
  - *Pediatric*: avoid; the immature liver is more susceptible to PA toxicity (paralleling the pennyroyal case — see _poejo.typ_).
  - *Concurrent hepatic-metabolism-relevant drugs*: theoretical interaction.
  - *Assa-peixe honey*: the honey from bees foraging on assa-peixe is a different pharmacological entity from the herbal preparation — the bee-mediated transfer of PAs is *modest but documented* in research on PA-contaminated honeys; the honey is generally considered safe at typical consumption levels but should not be the sole honey source for pregnancy / infant / hepatic-impairment use.#footnote[Pyrrolizidine alkaloids in _Vernonanthura polyanthes_: published Brazilian phytochemistry surveys; the PA presence is documented and is the principal modern safety overlay; verify against current Anvisa / Brazilian Pharmacopoeia guidance. Honey-PA transfer: standard apiculture and food-safety literature on PA-honey contamination from PA-producing plants (Senecio, Echium, and Vernonia genera).]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Not classical in Tibb._ Vernonanthura polyanthes _is exclusively Brazilian and was unknown to the medieval Arabic-Persian medical world; no canonical attestation. No Arabic name. Modern Arabic / Persian / Urdu commercial usage is the transliteration_ asabaixe / ассапейше. _Reasoned modern Unani placement on the bitter-respiratory-Asteraceae axis (entirely interpretive):_

*Mizāj* — by reasoned extension on the bitter-aromatic Asteraceae axis: *Hot and Dry in the 2nd degree* (paralleling chamomile *bābūnaj* and other respiratory-bitter Asteraceae in the Tibb framework).

*Af'āl* (reasoned): *munaffith / mukhrij al-balgham* (expectorant — the principal Brazilian indication, paralleling the canonical Tibb expectorant register); *muḥallil* (resolvent); *muqaṭṭiʿ li-l-balgham al-yābis* (cuts viscous phlegm); *muqawwī al-ṣadr* (chest tonic); *muḥsin al-tanaffus* (improves respiration); *jālī* (cleansing).

*Istemālāt* (reasoned): chronic bronchitis; cold-pattern asthma; chronic productive cough; respiratory tract congestion.

*Mawāne'*: hot-pattern conditions; pregnancy; hepatic disease (the PA caveat); Asteraceae allergy.

The substance does not appear in Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Khazāyin al-Adwiya. See _tibb-al-arabi.typ_.

