=== Brazilian / Indigenous / Caboclo — the substantive tradition

- *Chá de assa-peixe* — the canonical respiratory infusion: ~5-10 g of dried leaf in ~250 mL hot water, infused for 10-15 minutes; 1-2 cups daily for *short-term* respiratory complaint; *not for chronic / daily long-term use* (the PA caveat).
- *Xarope de assa-peixe* — assa-peixe-based syrup; commercial Brazilian phytotherapy product (Anvisa-registered) for respiratory complaints; the substance often combined with *guaco* (see _guaco.typ_), *mentruz* (_Lepidium virginicum_), and other Brazilian respiratory herbs in compound preparations.
- *Garrafada com assa-peixe* — Brazilian compound folk-medical decoction in a glass bottle (the broader *garrafada* tradition; cf. _zz-glossary.typ_); commonly with multiple other respiratory herbs.
- *Mel de assa-peixe* — *honey produced by bees foraging on assa-peixe*; the assa-peixe-blooming season (typically May-July in Southeast Brazil) yields a *dark, slightly-bitter, intensely-aromatic honey* with collector-grade status in Minas Gerais and São Paulo apiculture; sold as a Brazilian artisanal product; traditionally valued for respiratory support (the honey carrying a modest pharmacological-fraction transfer of the source plant chemistry).
- *Banho de assa-peixe* — bath / wash preparation; Afro-Brazilian / Candomblé / Umbanda ritual register in some traditions.
- *Tópico de folha macerada* — macerated leaves applied to wounds; the Asteraceae-topical-medicine register.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_V. polyanthes_ is principally a *tropical and subtropical Brazilian Cerrado-and-Atlantic-Forest* species; cultivation and traditional use are concentrated in Southeast and Central Brazil. See _climates.typ_.

*Subtropical-humid Brazilian Cerrado and Atlantic Forest (the canonical region).* The Cerrado climate carries hot wet summers, mild-to-cool dry winters (the distinctive seasonal-dryness register that distinguishes the Cerrado from the Amazonian and the coastal-Atlantic), and substantial UV; the assa-peixe plant produces its bitter-aromatic foliage as part of its insect-and-herbivore-deterrent profile and as a fire-resistance / drought-resistance strategy. In Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ terms the year carries _grīṣma_-_varṣā_ Pitta-Kapha load with a relatively dry _hemanta_-_śiśira_; in 中醫 terms the climate is 暑濕 (summer-heat-damp) on the Spleen-Heart and 燥 (dryness) in winter on the Lung; in Tibb terms the constitutional load shifts between Hot-Moist summer and Cold-Dry winter. *The traditional response and the assa-peixe register*: the bitter-warming-pungent character is constitutionally appropriate as a *cool-season respiratory drug* — *the winter-and-shoulder-season Cerrado cold-pattern bronchitis* that this climate generates is the canonical assa-peixe indication; summer-acute respiratory illness is also covered. The *assa-peixe-blooming season* (May-July, the early Brazilian dry winter) coincides with the typical respiratory-illness season in the Brazilian Southeast, and the *mel de assa-peixe* harvest is the climatically-appropriate seasonal-medicinal-food.

*Amazonian and Northeastern Brazilian (hot-humid).* Assa-peixe is less common in the Amazon proper; the Northeastern Brazilian (Caatinga, Northeastern Coastal) tradition uses other related Asteraceae species more commonly. The Northeast _V. condensata_ (*boldo-da-bahia*) and related substances have parallel but distinct uses.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Assa-peixe is rarely exported beyond Brazil — the substance is not part of the global ethnobotanical commerce in the way that guarana, açaí, and rapadura are. Modern Brazilian-diaspora communities in Portugal, the US, and elsewhere occasionally use the substance in small quantities. *The constitutional logic transposes well for the respiratory-bronchitis indication* in cool-damp climates (which constitutionally favor the same Kapha-pattern that the substance addresses); the *pyrrolizidine-alkaloid hepatotoxicity overlay transcends all climate questions* and is the principal modern safety constraint.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Sesquiterpene lactones* — hirsutinolides (the characteristic _Vernonia_-genus lactone class), glaucolides, vernolepin; the principal bitter-and-bioactive fraction.
- *Pyrrolizidine alkaloids (PAs)* — *the modern safety concern* — published Brazilian phytochemistry identifies 1,2-unsaturated PAs in _V. polyanthes_ at variable concentrations; PAs are *hepatotoxic via metabolic activation to pyrroles* that bind to liver proteins, causing centrilobular necrosis and hepatic veno-occlusive disease at chronic exposure. The PA register places assa-peixe in the same safety category as *comfrey* (_Symphytum officinale_), *coltsfoot* (_Tussilago farfara_), and *senecio* species — all PA-containing plants with documented hepatotoxic potential at chronic use.
- *Flavonoids* — apigenin, luteolin, quercetin glycosides.
- *Triterpenes* — α-amyrin, β-amyrin, lupeol, ursolic acid.
- *Essential oil (0.1-0.5%)* — caryophyllene, germacrene, α-humulene, sesquiterpenes characteristic of the genus.
- *Phenolic acids* — chlorogenic, caffeic acids.#footnote[Phytochemistry of _Vernonanthura polyanthes_: published Brazilian phytochemistry surveys in _Revista Brasileira de Farmacognosia_, _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytochemistry_; pyrrolizidine alkaloid surveys specifically (verify against current Anvisa monograph data).]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Expectorant / antitussive*: documented preclinical activity on respiratory smooth muscle and mucociliary clearance; the modern correlate of the Brazilian classical respiratory register. Clinical evidence is limited but the preclinical signal is consistent and the Brazilian-folk traditional use is universal.
- *Anti-inflammatory*: sesquiterpene-lactone-mediated; preclinical broad-spectrum activity (NF-κB and COX inhibition).
- *Antimicrobial*: in vitro broad-spectrum activity.
- *Bronchodilator*: documented in animal-model preparations; mechanism not fully characterized.
- *Antioxidant*: standard flavonoid antioxidant profile.
- *Wound-healing*: documented in animal models for topical preparations.
- *Anti-diabetic (preliminary)*: some preclinical evidence for hypoglycemic activity; clinically preliminary.
- *Pyrrolizidine hepatotoxicity*: the principal safety overlay — well-characterized in the broader PA literature; clinical translation to acute-use vs. chronic-use risk requires careful patient-and-dose consideration.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Brazilian-folk + reasoned-Tibb + modern preclinical expectorant-and-anti-inflammatory pharmacology converges cleanly on the respiratory register; the PA hepatotoxicity overlay is the principal modern caveat distinguishing this from the cleaner-safety profile of substances like _guaco_ (see _guaco.typ_).

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Dried leaf in infusion*: ~5-10 g per cup, 1-2 cups daily for *short-term* (1-2 weeks) respiratory complaint; *not for chronic / daily long-term use* (the PA accumulation concern).
- *Brazilian commercial xarope*: as per Anvisa-registered product labeling; short-term use.
- *Risk groups (absolute or relative contraindications)*:
  - *Pregnancy*: *contraindicated* (PA + Asteraceae register).
  - *Lactation*: *contraindicated* (PA transfer to milk).
  - *Pediatric*: *contraindicated for prolonged use*; short-term low-dose use under guidance.
  - *Hepatic disease*: *contraindicated*.
  - *Concurrent hepatic-metabolism-relevant drugs*: avoid.
  - *Asteraceae allergy*: avoid.
  - *Chronic long-term use*: *avoid in any patient* — the PA-accumulation concern is dose-and-duration dependent.
- *Mel de assa-peixe*: generally safe at typical dietary consumption; avoid as sole honey source in pregnancy, lactation, infants, and hepatic-impairment populations; reputable apiarist sourcing.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

